+# The Daily Grind
+
+Run `npm start` to serve the site.
+
+## Script performance diagnostics
+
+`script-performance.js` observes long animation frames (frames exceeding 50 ms)
+and aggregates script execution across callbacks by source URL. In browsers
+supporting the Long Animation Frames API, the console automatically shows scripts
+with at least 100 ms of cumulative execution in slow frames, batched every five
+seconds when new frames arrive.
+
+In DevTools:
+
+```js
+scriptPerformance.getReport() // All recorded scripts, slowest total duration first
+scriptPerformance.printReport({ minDuration: 50 }) // Print a custom threshold
+scriptPerformance.supported // Whether this browser supports attribution
+scriptPerformance.disconnect() // Stop recording; keep collected results
+```
+
+Each row includes the source URL, invocation count, total execution duration,
+longest invocation, and time spent forcing style/layout. All durations are in
+milliseconds. Results accumulate for the current page visit and stay local;
+no diagnostics are sent to a server.
+
+This measures script execution within slow frames, not download time or all
+JavaScript execution. Inline or unattributed scripts share a row. Browser security
+restrictions can omit cross-origin script attribution, so an empty report does not
+prove the absence of slow scripts. Unsupported browsers skip monitoring and log
+an informational message. The initial site contains no application scripts beyond
+this monitor, so an empty report is expected until slow script activity occurs.
+
+Run `npm test` for the monitor's automated checks.
